  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "If $y=e^{-x}\\cos x$, find $y'$ and $y''$, and show that $y''+2y'+2y=0$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$y'=-e^{-x}(\\cos x+\\sin x)$, $y''=2e^{-x}\\sin x$; hence $y''+2y'+2y=0$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=-e^{-x}\\cos x+e^{-x}(-\\sin x)\\\\&=-e^{-x}(\\cos x+\\sin x)\\\\y''&=e^{-x}(\\cos x+\\sin x)+(-e^{-x})(-\\sin x+\\cos x)\\\\&=e^{-x}(\\cos x+\\sin x)-e^{-x}(\\cos x-\\sin x)\\\\&=e^{-x}(2\\sin x)\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y''+2y'+2y&=2e^{-x}\\sin x+2(-e^{-x})(\\cos x+\\sin x)+2e^{-x}\\cos x\\\\&=2e^{-x}\\sin x-2e^{-x}\\cos x-2e^{-x}\\sin x+2e^{-x}\\cos x\\\\&=0\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 50
